{"tool_name":"genio","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"GenIO","home":"https://bioinformatics.ibioba-mpsp-conicet.gov.ar/GenIO/index.php","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Genomic Input Output (GenIO) identifies the most probable variants causing a rare disease, using the genomic and clinical information provided by a medical practitioner.","addition_date":"2019-05-10T06:47:52Z","last_update_date":"2020-06-16T10:55:23Z","tool_type":["Web application","Web service"],"version":["1.0"],"accessibility":"Restricted access","biotools_curie":"biotools:GenIO","cost":"Free of charge (with restrictions)","credit":[{"email":"genio@ibioba-mpsp-conicet.gov.ar","typeEntity":"Project","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://bioinformatics.ibioba-mpsp-conicet.gov.ar/GenIO/help.htm"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Exome assembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3229"},{"term":"Genotyping","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3196"}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"GenIO","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"Ruta","publication":[{"doi":"10.1186/s12859-018-2027-3","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2018 The Author(s).Background: GenIO is a novel web-server, designed to assist clinical genomics researchers and medical doctors in the diagnostic process of rare genetic diseases. The tool identifies the most probable variants causing a rare disease, using the genomic and clinical information provided by a medical practitioner. Variants identified in a whole-genome, whole-exome or target sequencing studies are annotated, classified and filtered by clinical significance. Candidate genes associated with the patient's symptoms, suspected disease and complementary findings are identified to obtain a small manageable number of the most probable recessive and dominant candidate gene variants associated with the rare disease case. Additionally, following the American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics and the Association of Molecular Pathology (ACMG-AMP) guidelines and recommendations, all potentially pathogenic variants that might be contributing to disease and secondary findings are identified. Results: A retrospective study was performed on 40 patients with a diagnostic rate of 40%. All the known genes that were previously considered as disease causing were correctly identified in the final inherit model output lists. In previously undiagnosed cases, we had no additional yield. Conclusion: This unique, intuitive and user-friendly tool to assists medical doctors in the clinical genomics diagnostic process is openly available at https://bioinformatics.ibioba-mpsp-conicet.gov.ar/GenIO/.","authors":[{"name":"Cordoba M."},{"name":"Kauffman M.A."},{"name":"Koile D."},{"name":"Yankilevich P."},{"name":"de Sousa Serro M."}],"citationCount":4,"date":"2018-01-27T00:00:00Z","journal":"BMC Bioinformatics","title":"GenIO: A phenotype-genotype analysis web server for clinical genomics of rare diseases"},"pmcid":"PMC5787240","pmid":"29374474","type":["Primary"],"version":"1.0"}],"topic":[{"term":"Exome sequencing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3676"},{"term":"Medical informatics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3063"},{"term":"Rare diseases","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3325"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"GenIO","home":"https://bio.tools/GenIO","license":"GPL-3.0","version":"1.0","summary":"Genomic Input Output (GenIO) identifies the most probable variants causing a rare disease, using the genomic and clinical information provided by a medical practitioner.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:genio/web/bioinformatics.ibioba-mpsp-conicet.gov.ar","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":true}}